Falsifies `system_tests::user::recv_cap_attack_tests::a_call_server_tells_its_reply_from_a_delegation_on_both_arrival_orders`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the tree as it stood until milestone 706 (a `CALL` server can tell a Reply from a
delegation), DECISIONS §245 (a `CALL` server tells a Reply from a delegation). The patch zeroes the
two stores of `abi::rendezvous::REPLY_DELIVERED` into the receiver's `x4`: `ipc_call_badged`'s
rendezvous arm (the server parked first) and `ipc_recv_cap`'s collect of a parked caller (the caller
parked first). With both at `0`, which is what every receive returned before 706, a `CALL`'s Reply
and a client's `SEND_CAP` delegation reach the server identically, a real slot in `x1` and `0` in
`x4`, and the server cannot tell them apart. The constant and `reply_tag` stay, so the patch
compiles; `#[allow(dead_code)]` keeps the kernel's lint quiet about the now unused helper.

Expected to fail: `assert!(wait_for(|| CALL_DONE.load(Ordering::SeqCst)), "the caller was never
answered (server_first = true): the server could not tell its Reply from the delegation (x4 = 0x0
on the CALL), so it deleted the Reply")`. Under the patch the server reads the Reply as a
delegation, deletes it as `user_mode_runtime::Delivered::into_reply` would, and its caller is never
answered. Replayed on 2026-10-03 (UTC) by the milestone 706 lane: red at exactly that assertion, on
the server-first order.

**The vacuity guards above the headline stay green under this patch**, which is what makes the red
mean the claim rather than the fixture: the server parks in `RECV_CAP`, takes the delegation and
parks again, and `SERVER_DONE` says it took both messages without hanging.

Replayed on aarch64; nothing here is architecture-specific. The tag is written in portable
scheduler code that all three ISAs share. The record is aarch64's because that is where
`script/falsifications` replays it, not because the defect is.

The diff's context cites milestone 599 (a frame per filesystem client channel) and milestone 634
(a plain SEND received by RECV_CAP never hands the receiver a sender-chosen slot).

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched.rs b/kernel/src/sched.rs
--- a/kernel/src/sched.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched.rs
@@ -3658,6 +3658,7 @@
 /// the receiver's table was full and there is no Reply to name. Written only on the two paths a
 /// `CALL` reaches a receiver (`ipc_call_badged`'s rendezvous and `ipc_recv_cap`'s collect of a
 /// parked caller), so a `SEND_CAP` delegation, which reaches the same `x1`, never carries it.
+#[allow(dead_code)]
 #[inline(always)]
 fn reply_tag(slot: u64) -> u64 {
     if slot == NO_CAP {
@@ -3795,7 +3796,7 @@
                     // SEND_CAP, whose sender parked mailbox[1] = 0), x3 = the sender's badge (msg[3],
                     // milestone 599), x4 = REPLY_DELIVERED iff x1 is this caller's Reply (milestone
                     // 706), else 0. Never BOUND: that is the arm above.
-                    let tag = if is_reply { reply_tag(slot) } else { 0 };
+                    let tag = 0;
                     Some([msg[0], slot, msg[1], msg[3], tag])
                 }
                 inter_process_communication::Recv::Blocked => {
@@ -3917,7 +3918,7 @@
                 // value instead of a zero, so the server's RECV_CAP surfaces which client called.
                 // Word 4 says x1 is a Reply (milestone 706), which only this path and the collect
                 // in `ipc_recv_cap` may say; a SEND_CAP's delivery leaves it 0.
-                r.mailbox = [msg[0], slot, msg[1], badge, reply_tag(slot)];
+                r.mailbox = [msg[0], slot, msg[1], badge, 0];
                 // A Reply capability was installed, so RECV_CAP's x1 is a real slot (milestone 634).
                 r.cap_delivered = true;
                 r.handshake.serve(); // delivered: this wake passes the boot-8 gate
